const firstTimeFaqs = [
  { question: "What do I need to bring to a Maine dispensary on my first visit?", answer: "Bring one valid government-issued photo ID showing you are 21 or older — Maine accepts a driver's license, passport, or state ID from any US state. Maine dispensaries do not require a medical card for adult-use purchases. If you are a registered medical patient from Maine or a reciprocity state, also bring your medical card to access the medical program (saves the 14% adult-use excise tax). Payment is cash or debit — most Maine dispensaries do not accept credit cards because of banking restrictions on the cannabis industry." },
  { question: "Can I go to a Maine dispensary if I'm from out of state?", answer: "Yes. Maine's adult-use program allows any adult 21+ with valid photo ID from any US state to purchase at any licensed adult-use dispensary. Out-of-state residents do not pay a special non-resident tax — the 5.5% state sales tax and 14% adult-use excise tax apply equally. If you hold a valid medical cannabis card from one of the 28 OCP-approved states, you can additionally use it at Maine registered dispensaries (often a better value). See the Out-of-State Medical Cannabis Patients in Maine reciprocity guide for the full details." },
  { question: "What happens when I walk in — is it like a pharmacy?", answer: "Not exactly. Maine dispensaries typically have a reception area where you show ID, then either enter the retail floor directly or wait in a lobby before being admitted by a budtender. The reception staff verifies your age and lets you in. Many stores have security cameras, locked display cases, and product menus on tablets or printed sheets rather than self-service browsing. The budtender (the staff member who helps you) is trained to walk you through products, recommend starting doses, and answer questions. Tell them it is your first visit — they will slow down and explain." },
  { question: "How much cannabis can I buy at one time in Maine?", answer: "Under Title 28-B §1501, a Maine adult 21+ can purchase up to 2.5 ounces of cannabis or a combination of cannabis and cannabis concentrate that includes no more than 10 grams of concentrate per transaction. Maine dispensaries also impose their own per-transaction limits (typically 1-2 ounces for first-time buyers) — but you can return the same day for additional purchases as long as you're under the 2.5-ounce cap. Possession limits in public are the same: 2.5 ounces total. There is no legal limit on how much you can store at home." },
  { question: "Should I tell the budtender it's my first time?", answer: "Yes — explicitly. Maine budtenders are trained to handle first-time customers and will slow their recommendations, explain dose basics, and steer you away from products that produce the most common first-time negative experiences (very high-THC concentrates, untested edibles, etc.). Many Maine dispensaries also offer first-time-buyer promotions — ask at the register or check the operator's website before you go. Telling the budtender your experience level also helps them recommend products for what you actually want (relaxation, sleep, social, focus) rather than just selling you what's most expensive on the menu." },
  { question: "How do I choose between flower, edibles, vapes, and tinctures on my first visit?", answer: "Different formats produce different effects on different timelines. The choice depends on what you want and how much time you have to evaluate the effect: Inhaled (flower or vape) — onset in 5-15 minutes, duration 2-3 hours, easy to titrate by waiting between puffs. Best for first-time buyers who want predictable control. Edibles (gummies, chocolates, baked goods) — onset 60-120 minutes, duration 4-8 hours, harder to titrate because the effect arrives all at once. Maine caps a single serving at 10mg THC and a package at 200mg THC. Best for users who want a long, sustained effect and are willing to wait. Tinctures (sublingual) — onset 15-30 minutes, duration 2-4 hours, easy to titrate via dropper. Best for users who want predictable dose control without smoking or vaping. For a true first visit where you want to evaluate effect within the same day, inhaled or sublingual is the most predictable format." },
  { question: "How do I pay, and is there a tip expectation?", answer: "Payment: cash or debit (PIN-based) — most Maine dispensaries do not accept credit cards. Some have on-site ATMs (with typical $2-3 ATM fees). Maine imposes a 5.5% state sales tax on every cannabis purchase plus a 14% adult-use excise tax (effective 2026, raised from 10% per PL 2023 c. 2). The budtender usually adds these at the register and shows the post-tax total before you pay. Tipping is appreciated but not expected — most Maine dispensaries do not have a digital tipping system, so cash tips in the budtender's tip jar are the norm. $5-10 for a routine visit is generous; skip if cash is tight." },
  { question: "Can I consume cannabis at the dispensary or in public?", answer: "No. Maine law prohibits public consumption of cannabis (Title 28-B §1501(2)). Consumption is restricted to private residences (including curtilage) and explicitly-permitted private property. You cannot consume in your car, on the sidewalk outside the dispensary, in a hotel room (most hotels prohibit it regardless), in a state or national park, or in any public space. The Maine adult-use program does not have licensed consumption lounges as of 2026, although several operator proposals are pending. Smoking in a vehicle is a civil violation regardless of consumption; driving under the influence of cannabis is a criminal offense (OUI) with per-se THC blood limits." },
];

